1. What It Is
A provenance-aware, versioned registry of psychological effects. A psychological effect is a claimed relationship between a manipulation or condition and a measurable psychological outcome: the ego depletion effect, the power posing effect, the marshmallow test, the Dunning-Kruger effect, the facial feedback hypothesis, the priming effect, and many others. The Psychological Effect Registry records each effect with its name, its description, the original study, the effect size, the replications, the replication status, the revisions, and the provenance.

It reuses:

the Physics Knowledge Library (App 23)

the Replication Tracker (App 26)

the Full-Pipeline Simulator (App 9)

the Null-Model Protocol Library (App 18)

the Provenance-Aware Annotation Database (App 20)

the PROV-O Exporter (App 3)

the Revision History Viewer (App 7)

the Numerical Verification Tool (App 22)

the Evidence Aggregator (App 46)

the Autofill Library Service (App 49)

It is for psychologists, replication researchers, journal editors, science journalists, students, and anyone who wants to know which psychological effects have held up.

2. Core Requirements
Functional requirements:

Accept deposits of psychological effects from users.

Store each effect with its name, its description, the original study, and its provenance.

Record the original study: the authors, the year, the journal, the sample size, the effect size, and the confidence interval.

Record the replications: the attempts to reproduce the effect, their sample sizes, their effect sizes, and their outcomes.

Record the replication status: not_replicated, replicated, partially_replicated, failed_replication, or uncertain.

Record the effect size in the original study and in the replications.

Record the p-value in the original study and in the replications.

Record the meta-analytic effect size, if one has been computed.

Record the moderators: the conditions under which the effect appears or disappears.

Record the boundary conditions: the conditions under which the effect does not appear.

Record the theoretical basis: the theory that predicts the effect.

Record the practical significance: whether the effect matters in practice.

Enforce provenance completeness.

Enforce versioning.

Enforce supersession acyclicity.

Support querying by effect ID, name, domain, replication status, original author, year, depositor, deposit date, and keywords.

Support full-text search of the description, the original study, and the notes.

Support aggregation queries: count by domain, by replication status, by original author, by year, by journal.

Support exporting an effect as JSON, PROV-O, and Turtle.

Support importing an effect from JSON.

Support bulk import and bulk export.

Support a REST API for CRUD operations.

Support a GraphQL API for flexible queries.

Support a command-line interface for administration.

Support authentication and authorization.

Support audit logging.

Support backup and restore.

Support a web interface.

Support moderation.

Support linking an effect to a Physics Knowledge Library work if the effect is described in a deposited work.

Support linking an effect to a Replication Tracker record.

Support linking an effect to a Full-Pipeline Simulator result if the effect has been tested statistically.

Support linking an effect to a Null-Model Protocol result.

Support linking an effect to a Numerical Verification Tool result.

Support the effect map: a visualisation of effects by domain and by replication status.

Support the effect size comparison view: a visualisation that compares the original effect size with the replication effect sizes.

Support the replication funnel: a visualisation of the path from the original study to the replication.

Support the effect timeline: a visualisation of how the effect's status has changed over time.

Support the forest plot: a visualisation of the effect sizes across studies.

Non-functional requirements:

Deterministic.

Fast: query a typical effect in under 100 milliseconds.

Scalable: support up to 10^6 effects.

Versioned: the registry records the STAG schema version, the Null-Model Protocol schema version, the psychological effect schema version, and the registry version.

Reproducible.

Portable.

Well-documented.

